  "rationale": "The example has one `{test}` reference. It is in `tests/definitions.rs`, on `fn globs_exclude_terms_but_capture_referenced_terms_outside_the_glob` (marked at line 128), and the test also cites `requirements.definitions.selection`.\n\n**Setup**\n- `fixture()` creates the requirement `rules.ready`. It references `{term}glossary.a`, which in turn references `glossary.b`. Both are definitions in a different namespace (`glossary`) that the selection does not match.\n- The test appends an unreferenced definition `rules.unrelated` to `md/rules.md`. Its name matches the selection's prefix.\n- `capture()` adds the input with the selection `rules.*`. This stands in for the example's `requirements.cli.*`. The structure is the same: a requirement, a referenced definition outside the glob, and an unreferenced definition inside the glob's name space.\n\n**Assertions**\n- `inputs[0].matches == [\"rules.ready\"]`. The glob selected only the requirement, and the unreferenced `rules.unrelated` definition was not selected despite matching the name prefix.\n- `inputs[1].definition == \"glossary.a\"` and `inputs[2].definition == \"glossary.b\"`. The referenced definition outside the glob, and its transitive dependency, were captured as separate definition inputs.\n- `status_markdown(...)` does not contain `rules.unrelated`. `status_markdown_with_options` skips `DefinitionKind::Term` entries, so this only shows that the unrelated definition is not listed as a status row.\n\n**Gap**\n- The test does not assert the total input count, so it never directly rules out a fourth input for `rules.unrelated`. The exact `matches` list on the first input is the main evidence that the name-prefix match did not make it a dependency.\n- I judge this too minor to block the example.\n\nThe setup and assertions cover the example's scenario and expected outcomes.",
